//
// Phase E1: the sweep plans configurations the builders can actually build.
//
// sweep_families.METHODS duplicates two things it does not own: the tile_m each
// GEMM method forces, and how a configuration's micro-kernel object is named. It
// duplicates them so the sweep can plan without importing the builders, and
// because it also knows `direct`, which gemm_method_spec deliberately raises on.
//
// A duplicate is only safe while something checks it, and the failure mode of a
// drift is expensive rather than obvious: _spec_with_tiles asserts the registry's
// tile_m equals the method's, so a drifted row does not mis-tune a model, it
// aborts one at build time -- hours into a gate, several frames inside aircc.
// This is the cheap place for that to fail instead.
//
// It also pins the Phase E1 property at the PLANNING end: no two tile_n the sweep
// may elect for one method share an object name. mm_aie2p.cc bakes tile_n in as a
// -D flag, so two configurations of one method at different tile_n are two
// different micro-kernels; naming them the same means one silently overwrites the
// other and aiecc links whichever landed last. sweep_families may plan seven
// tile_n values for one method, so it is not hypothetical -- and the hardware
// half of it, Phase D2's zeroed 32-of-128 output columns, cost a whole gate cycle
// to localize.
//
// No NPU, no XRT, no Peano: two Python tables and a naming function. It sits
// beside the registry-writer tests for the same reason those do, and it is cheap
// enough to keep in a PR check.
//
// The test file carries no framework dependency -- pytest is not installed in
// the sandbox venv -- so it runs as a plain script and prints a pass count. The
// count is part of the gate: a file that silently stopped defining a test would
// still print a final line saying everything passed.
//
// Editing this header: lit scans the WHOLE file for its directives, so naming
// one in prose -- even inside a comment -- registers as a second directive and
// leaves the test UNRESOLVED. Describe them, never spell them out.
//
// RUN: python3 %S/test_sweep_families.py | FileCheck %s
//
// CHECK: sweep families tests: 6/6 passed
